{
  "_meta": {
    "title": "Crafting-lineage acquisition for items with no recorded drop",
    "issue": 285,
    "harvested": "2026-08-29",
    "source": "ddowiki.com item pages, batched MediaWiki API (prop=revisions), per docs/wiki-evidence/harvest-method.md",
    "why": "#262's triage found 179 items whose wiki page records an acquisition source the dataset lacks. They are not unobtainable — they are CRAFTED from a predecessor, and the wiki records which one in the `{{Named item}}` template's `epic` / `legendary` parameters. Without this they render under 'Source unknown' with no way to learn how to get them.",
    "params": "`epic = <base item>` means the page IS the epic-crafted version of that base. `legendary = <epic predecessor>` means it is the Legendary Crafting upgrade of that one.",
    "flag_value_guard": "The values no/none/yes are template FLAGS, not lineage. #262's triage recorded that `Legendary Great Forge Helm` and `Legendary High Priestess' Focus` carry `epic = no` and were initially misread as sourced; the harvest that produced this shard applies the same guard, and none of the 177 entries came from a flag value.",
    "one_step_only": "Each entry records ONE step, verbatim as the wiki states it. A Legendary item points at its Epic predecessor, which has its own entry pointing at the base. The chain is followed by composing entries, never by resolving it here — collapsing three wiki statements into one would publish a claim no single page makes.",
    "not_a_derivation": "Every value is harvested. The naming pattern is perfectly regular across all 177 (Epic X -> X, Legendary X -> Epic X), and `tests/test_crafting_lineage.py` asserts that — NOT to derive from, but so a future entry that breaks the pattern is caught rather than trusted."
  },
